mplete. This is an infinite loop.\nI need to completely rethink the game. Let me consider:\n1. Maybe the goal is NOT to click green blocks at all\n2. Maybe the goal is to move the player to a specific position\n3. Maybe the goal is to use ACTION7 without clicking green blocks\n4. Maybe the goal is to click the player or some other object\nLooking at the current board, I see:\n- Player in blue corridor (left side)\n- Blue regions\n- Black areas on left and right\n- No green blocks\nThe player is at the left side of the blue corridor. In the initial state, the player was also at the left side. Maybe the goal is to move the player to the RIGHT side of the corridor?\nLet me try moving the player all the way to the right and see if that completes the level.\nActually, wait. Let me reconsider the game from scratch. The initial state had:\n- 7 green blocks\n- Player at left\n- Blue regions\nThe human solution is about 21 actions. If the goal is to move the player from left to right, that could be many LEFT/RIGHT actions. But 21 actions for a simple left-to-right movement seems like a lot.\nAlternatively, maybe the goal is to click all green blocks (7 clicks) and then move the player to a specific position (14 more moves = 21 total). But I already tried clicking all green blocks and the level didn't complete.\nLet me try a completely different approach: move the player to the right side of the corridor and see what happens.", "kind": "reasoning", "inContext": false}, {"label": "TOOL CALL: python", "content": "<tool_call>\n<function=python>\n<parameter=code>\n# Let me try moving the player to the right side of the corridor\n# First check player position\nseg = current_frame.segmentation\nplayer = [n for n in seg['nodes'] if n['color'] == 'b'][0]\nprint(f\"Player: {player['boundary']}\")\n\n# Check blue region to understand corridor\nblue = [n for n in seg['nodes'] if n['color'] == 'S']\nprint(f\"Blue regions: {len(blue)}\")\nfor b in blue:\n    print(f\"  pixels={b['pixels']